Round 1 Incident Report

Executive Summary

Assessor cracked a weak password (bcarter) with a spray attack, got into a file share, and pulled a test file with fake NPI in it. No escalation, no lateral movement. Fixed: lockout policy, password requirements, file auditing, and a Splunk alert for spray patterns.

What happened

An external assessor sprayed passwords against three accounts (bcarter, lreyes, smoore) over SMB. bcarter had a weak password and fell after about 14,000 attempts; the other two held. The assessor reports copying a file off the Customer Information share, I can confirm the login, but not the file access itself, since Object Access auditing wasn't on yet for that share.

Source IP: 10.10.10.200
Account compromised: bcarter
Attack window: 12:39 PM – 1:16 PM
Scope

No group changes, no new accounts, no privilege changes. Stayed contained to bcarter's existing access.

Red team vs. my logs

Their log shows ~35 minutes of recon before the spray (nmap, enumeration, a failed AS-REP roast) that never shows up in Splunk, the Sysmon config filters most network events by default. Their reported times also run 10-15 minutes behind what Splunk logged, likely clock drift or a reused session. Their own notes on what should've alerted matched what I found independently, good sign.

Fixing
Account lockout back on
Stronger password policy
File access auditing on sensitive shares
Splunk alert for failed-logon bursts
Tune Sysmon to actually catch network connections

MITRE mapping

T1110.003 (Password Spraying)
Mitigations: M1036, M1027
Detection: DET0487 
  AN1336, high volume of auth failures across multiple accounts in a short window.
